WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.707
Ethanol, the alcohol in this bottle, has the

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.360
formula C two H six O.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.960
Methoxymethane, used as a propellant in some spray

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.200
cans, has exactly the same formula: two carbons,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.360
six hydrogens, one oxygen.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.480
Yet ethanol boils at seventy eight degrees, and

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.200
methoxymethane at about minus twenty five, so it's

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.000
a gas at room temperature.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.680
Same atoms, different substances.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.080
So how can one formula make different molecules?

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.260
A molecular formula only counts the atoms.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.260
It doesn't show how they're joined.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.220
In ethanol, the oxygen sits at the end

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.220
of the chain with a hydrogen on it:

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.580
an O H group.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.260
In methoxymethane, the oxygen sits between the two

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.380
carbons.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.500
Compounds with the same molecular formula, but their

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.980
atoms joined in a different order, are called

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.500
structural isomers.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.700
They're different compounds, with different names and different

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:58.420
properties.

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.620
In the last video, every name pointed to

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:02.500
one structure.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.460
Now one formula can point to several.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.186
And the arrangement changes the properties.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:12.586
That O H lets ethanol molecules hydrogen bond

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.266
to each other, which you met in Year

00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:17.466
11: the hydrogen on one molecule's oxygen is

00:01:17.547 --> 00:01:19.706
attracted to the oxygen of the next.

00:01:20.027 --> 00:01:23.546
Methoxymethane has no hydrogen on its oxygen, so

00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:26.106
its molecules can't hydrogen bond to each other.

00:01:26.587 --> 00:01:28.666
They have the same atoms, so they're about

00:01:28.707 --> 00:01:29.786
the same size.

00:01:30.187 --> 00:01:32.186
The hydrogen bonds make the difference.

00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:35.786
Weaker attractions between molecules mean a lower boiling

00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:36.266
point.

00:01:36.987 --> 00:01:39.626
Isomers come in three kinds, and the kind

00:01:39.787 --> 00:01:41.306
depends on what changed.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:44.293
First, chain isomers.

00:01:44.374 --> 00:01:48.053
The carbon skeleton changes, but the family, here

00:01:48.094 --> 00:01:50.053
the alkanes, stays the same.

00:01:50.694 --> 00:01:52.853
Butane is four carbons in a row.

00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:54.933
Take a carbon off the end and hang

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.653
it on the middle, and you get two-methylpropane.

00:01:58.134 --> 00:02:00.533
Both are C four H ten, both are

00:02:00.614 --> 00:02:01.253
alkanes.

00:02:01.814 --> 00:02:04.613
Camping gas often contains both, and they boil

00:02:04.694 --> 00:02:05.813
at different temperatures.

00:02:06.214 --> 00:02:09.013
The next video explains why branching does that.

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:12.513
Second, position isomers.

00:02:12.794 --> 00:02:15.033
The skeleton stays the same, and so does

00:02:15.114 --> 00:02:16.153
the family.

00:02:16.314 --> 00:02:18.953
Only the group moves to a different carbon.

00:02:19.034 --> 00:02:21.913
In but-one-ene, the double bond starts at carbon

00:02:22.074 --> 00:02:22.793
one.

00:02:22.874 --> 00:02:25.833
In but-two-ene it starts at carbon two.

00:02:25.914 --> 00:02:28.153
The same goes for an O H group.

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:30.873
The ending ol means an O H, which

00:02:30.954 --> 00:02:33.353
is why ethanol ends in ol, and the

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:37.193
number says which carbon carries it: propan-one-ol and

00:02:37.274 --> 00:02:40.193
propan-two-ol are position isomers too.

00:02:40.554 --> 00:02:42.953
Moving a group can change how it reacts,

00:02:43.034 --> 00:02:45.193
and you'll see later that these two alcohols

00:02:45.274 --> 00:02:46.633
give different products.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.233
Third, functional group isomers.

00:02:50.314 --> 00:02:52.793
A functional group is the part of a

00:02:52.834 --> 00:02:56.073
molecule that gives its family its reactions, like

00:02:56.154 --> 00:02:58.153
a double bond, or an O H.

00:02:58.794 --> 00:03:01.753
In these isomers, the group itself is different.

00:03:02.394 --> 00:03:05.513
Ethanol and methoxymethane are one pair.

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:07.673
Here's a pair of hydrocarbons.

00:03:07.914 --> 00:03:10.873
But-one-ene is C four H eight, with a

00:03:10.954 --> 00:03:11.593
double bond.

00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:14.793
Join the four carbons into a ring instead,

00:03:14.874 --> 00:03:16.553
and you get cyclobutane.

00:03:17.034 --> 00:03:19.193
It's also C four H eight, but it

00:03:19.274 --> 00:03:21.513
has only single bonds, so it's in a

00:03:21.594 --> 00:03:25.833
different family: the cycloalkanes, rings of carbons with

00:03:25.994 --> 00:03:27.473
only single bonds.

00:03:27.754 --> 00:03:30.953
One is unsaturated, the other saturated.

00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:35.206
And a different group means different chemistry.

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:37.846
The double bond is where an alkene reacts,

00:03:37.927 --> 00:03:41.446
so cyclobutane, with no double bond, won't react

00:03:41.527 --> 00:03:42.726
the same way.

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:45.606
That's why chemists care which isomer they have,

00:03:45.687 --> 00:03:47.766
and later you'll meet a simple test that

00:03:47.847 --> 00:03:49.446
tells these two apart.

00:03:49.487 --> 00:03:52.086
So the group sets the family's reactions, and

00:03:52.127 --> 00:03:54.166
the full arrangement sets the name.

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:58.966
So, to classify a pair, ask three questions.

00:03:59.447 --> 00:04:00.726
Same molecular formula?

00:04:01.127 --> 00:04:03.366
If not, they aren't isomers at all.

00:04:03.847 --> 00:04:08.326
Butane and but-one-ene, for example, aren't isomers: but-one-ene

00:04:08.407 --> 00:04:10.286
has two fewer hydrogens.

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:11.366
Different family?

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:13.606
Then they're functional group isomers.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:14.886
Same family?

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:17.846
Then check whether the skeleton changed, or only

00:04:17.887 --> 00:04:18.886
the group's position.

00:04:19.287 --> 00:04:23.766
Watch the classic slip: propan-one-ol and propan-two-ol aren't

00:04:23.847 --> 00:04:25.126
functional group isomers.

00:04:25.447 --> 00:04:26.566
Both are alcohols.

00:04:26.727 --> 00:04:28.966
The group moved, it didn't change.

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:32.966
To find every isomer of a formula, work

00:04:33.127 --> 00:04:34.006
systematically.

00:04:34.487 --> 00:04:36.886
For C five H twelve, start with the

00:04:36.967 --> 00:04:39.046
longest chain: pentane.

00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:41.526
Shorten the chain by one, and put that

00:04:41.607 --> 00:04:44.326
carbon back as a branch on carbon two:

00:04:44.407 --> 00:04:45.926
two-methylbutane.

00:04:46.247 --> 00:04:48.886
Shorten it again, and both spare carbons go

00:04:48.927 --> 00:04:52.646
on the middle carbon: two, two-dimethylpropane.

00:04:52.967 --> 00:04:55.766
Shorten once more, to two carbons, and any

00:04:55.927 --> 00:04:57.846
branch you add just makes a longer chain

00:04:57.927 --> 00:04:58.726
again.

00:04:58.887 --> 00:05:00.006
So that's all three.

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:02.913
But watch for duplicates.

00:05:03.154 --> 00:05:05.313
Bending a chain on the page doesn't make

00:05:05.354 --> 00:05:08.193
a new molecule: this bent drawing is still

00:05:08.354 --> 00:05:08.993
pentane.

00:05:09.394 --> 00:05:11.553
A methyl on an end carbon just makes

00:05:11.634 --> 00:05:15.713
the chain longer, so one-methylbutane is pentane again.

00:05:15.874 --> 00:05:19.713
And three-methylbutane is two-methylbutane counted from the wrong

00:05:19.794 --> 00:05:20.113
end.

00:05:20.514 --> 00:05:22.753
The test is to name every structure.

00:05:23.154 --> 00:05:25.073
Same name, same molecule.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.940
So remember: same formula, different arrangement.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.340
Isomers always have the same atoms, so check

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.940
the formula first.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.540
Then ask what changed: the chain, the position,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:39.260
or the group.

00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:41.346
Your turn.

00:05:41.587 --> 00:05:45.346
Classify each pair as chain, position or functional

00:05:45.427 --> 00:05:46.746
group isomers.

00:05:47.027 --> 00:05:49.746
Pent-one-ene and pent-two-ene.

00:05:49.987 --> 00:05:53.266
Pentane and two, two-dimethylpropane.

00:05:53.507 --> 00:05:55.986
Cyclopentane and pent-one-ene.

00:05:56.387 --> 00:05:57.666
Pause here to answer.

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:11.386
First pair: same chain, same double bond, it

00:06:11.427 --> 00:06:14.186
just moved, so position isomers.

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:17.986
Second pair: both alkanes, with a different skeleton,

00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:19.346
so chain isomers.

00:06:19.827 --> 00:06:23.426
Cyclopentane is a cycloalkane, a ring with no

00:06:23.507 --> 00:06:26.066
double bond, so it's a different family from

00:06:26.147 --> 00:06:28.946
pent-one-ene: functional group isomers.

00:06:29.187 --> 00:06:31.586
Calling the first pair chain isomers is the

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:32.146
slip.

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:34.066
The chain didn't change.

00:06:35.267 --> 00:06:35.906
Last one.

00:06:36.147 --> 00:06:39.346
A student draws these three structures, and says

00:06:39.427 --> 00:06:41.986
they show three different isomers of C five

00:06:42.227 --> 00:06:43.266
H twelve.

00:06:43.587 --> 00:06:45.546
Evaluate the student's statement.

00:06:45.827 --> 00:06:47.186
Pause here to answer.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.760
Name each one, and trace the longest chain.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.040
A and B are both pentane.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.480
B is the same chain drawn with a

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:06.240
bend.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.480
C is two-methylbutane.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.840
So the statement is incorrect: the drawings show

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.680
only two different isomers.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.680
The third, two, two-dimethylpropane, isn't drawn.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.640
Counting a bent drawing as a new molecule

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:21.680
is the slip.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.620
So isomers share a formula, but not a

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:28.740
structure, and the structure decides the name and

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:29.540
the properties.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:34.420
Next: how carbon's bonds give hydrocarbons their shapes,

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.900
and why chain length and branching change their

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:38.020
boiling points.

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:40.673
Here are the key notes.

00:07:40.914 --> 00:07:42.833
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:42.914 --> 00:07:43.713
to copy them.
